## Cause

The problem occurs because you're passing a regular function (rather than an arrow function) to `setInterval`. When a regular function is invoked, it receives its own `this` binding. Since `setInterval` calls your function in a context without an explicit object reference, `this` inside the function doesn't refer to your `Timer` instance but instead points to the global object (or is `undefined` if you're in strict mode).

Since `this.seconds` doesn't exist on the global object, the expression `undefined + 1` evaluates to `NaN`, which is why the output shows `NaN` on every interval tick.

## Fix

Replace your regular function with an arrow function. Arrow functions don't create their own `this` binding; instead, they inherit `this` from their enclosing lexical scope—in this case, your `Timer` instance:

```javascript
class Timer {
  constructor() {
    this.seconds = 0;
  }
  start() {
    setInterval(() => {
      this.seconds += 1;
      console.log(this.seconds);
    }, 1000);
  }
}
new Timer().start();
```

### Other ways to fix it

If you prefer to keep a regular function, two alternative approaches work:

- **Store `this` in a separate variable:**
  ```javascript
  start() {
    const self = this;
    setInterval(function () {
      self.seconds += 1;
      console.log(self.seconds);
    }, 1000);
  }
  ```
- **Explicitly bind the `this` value:**
  ```javascript
  start() {
    setInterval(function () {
      this.seconds += 1;
      console.log(this.seconds);
    }.bind(this), 1000);
  }
  ```

For contemporary JavaScript, the arrow function approach is the preferred solution because it's both the most straightforward and the most idiomatic.

